{
  "image_name": "aba298ebda605bbcb4da2383a350fabc.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes several transverse transport effects in a sample and organizes them by what drives them and what kind of response they produce. The horizontal direction contrasts electrically driven effects on the left with thermally driven effects on the right, while the vertical direction separates charge responses above from non-charge responses below.\n\nAt the center is the nonlinear Hall effect: an oscillating electric field produces a transverse current at twice the frequency and a measured second-harmonic voltage, alongside a longitudinal current at the original frequency. Around it are four related effects. In the upper left, the Magnus effect appears to come from a spatial potential variation and an AC current, giving an AC transverse voltage. In the upper right, the thermoelectric case shows a temperature gradient driving a DC transverse charge current and voltage. In the lower left, a spin-related effect produces a DC spin current and voltage under electrical excitation, illustrated with opposite spin directions. In the lower right, a purely thermal response is shown: a temperature gradient drives a heat current and a temperature difference rather than charge flow.\n\nOverall, the diagram highlights a family of analogous transverse responses linking electrical, thermal, spin, and nonlinear phenomena.",
